sections.s2_technical:
Strong consistency means that once a write commits, any subsequent read anywhere returns that value or newer, never an older one. Achieving it in a distributed system requires coordination, such as writing through a single leader, using quorum reads and writes, or synchronous replication, all of which add latency and can reduce availability during partitions. For a bank ledger this cost is worth it, because serving a stale balance could permit spending money twice.

```java
// Strong: read goes to the leader (or a quorum) → always the latest committed value
public BigDecimal balanceStrong(String accountId) {
    return leader.read(accountId);   // never a stale replica for money
}
```

Eventual consistency relaxes that guarantee. A write is accepted and propagated asynchronously to replicas, so for a short window different replicas may hold different values, but given no new writes they all converge. This allows low-latency, highly available reads from any replica and massive scale, which is why social feeds, view counts, and recommendation data use it. The staleness window is usually milliseconds to seconds and is harmless for such data.

```java
// Eventual: read from any nearby replica → fast, possibly slightly stale
public long likeCountEventual(String postId) {
    return nearestReplica.read(postId);   // may lag; converges shortly
}
```

Between these extremes are useful middle grounds. Read-your-writes consistency guarantees a user sees their own writes immediately even if others see them slightly later, which I apply so a customer always sees their own just-made change. Causal consistency preserves cause-and-effect ordering. Tunable consistency, offered by databases like Cassandra, lets you choose per operation how many replicas must acknowledge, trading latency for freshness.

The key design skill is matching the consistency level to the data, often within the same system. A bank uses strong consistency for the ledger and balances, but can use eventual consistency for a dashboard of trending features, aggregate statistics, or a community feed. A senior nuance is that strong consistency trades availability and latency for correctness, as the CAP theorem formalizes, so I apply it only where a wrong or stale value causes real harm, and I deliberately choose eventual consistency elsewhere to gain speed, scale, and availability.

